Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex re(R"(#)");
    std::regex_replace(html, re, "<h1>$1</h1>");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Sample Heading\n\nThis is a sample paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace the `#` character in the markdown text with an HTML `<h1>` tag. The `std::regex` class is used to define the regular expression, and the `std::regex_replace` function is used to perform the replacement. The `\1` in the replacement string refers to the capturing group matched by the regular expression, which is the text following the `#` character.

The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by converting a sample markdown string to HTML and printing the result.

